# .invariants.example
#
# This file is a template. Copy it to .invariants and fill it in.
# Delete this comment block when you're done.
#
# PURPOSE
# -------
# This file declares what must remain true about this repository.
# It is not documentation. It is not aspirational.
# Every assert in this file is a claim you are prepared to defend —
# and that the watcher will verify automatically.
#
# WHO OWNS THIS FILE
# ------------------
# The maintainer of this repo. Not the agent. Not CI.
# If you merge a change that breaks an assert, that's a signal,
# not a bug in the watcher.
#
# WHEN TO UPDATE THIS FILE
# ------------------------
# - When the repo's responsibilities change
# - When a dependency contract changes
# - When a deadline miss or incident reveals an assumption
#   that was never written down
# - NOT after a PR is already open. Before.
#
# RULE OF THUMB FOR WRITING ASSERTS
# ----------------------------------
# A good assert is falsifiable. Ask yourself:
#   "Would I know within 24 hours if this stopped being true?"
# If yes — it belongs here.
# If it's too vague to fail — it doesn't belong here.
#
# BAD:  "The code is well structured"         ← not falsifiable
# GOOD: "No component imports directly from   ← falsifiable, verifiable
#        the contracts layer — all access
#        goes through the SDK"
#
# SEVERITY LEVELS
# ---------------
# FROZEN     — any change forks the protocol. Do not touch without a fork decision.
# VERSIONED  — can change, but requires a migration plan before the PR lands.
# ADDITIVE   — new surface only. Nothing existing breaks.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HEADER — fill these in
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

version: "1.0"

scope: "your-org.your-repo-name"
# Convention: org-name.repo-name
# Example:    open-creator-rails.demo

inherits: "../.invariants"
# Point to the parent .invariants
# The parent .invariants file may live outside of Github entirely. If unsure, leave this line as is.

maintainer: "@github-handle"
# The person who owns this file. One person. If it's everyone, it's no one.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ASSERTS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

asserts:

  # ── EXAMPLE 1: Dependency boundary ──────────────────────────────────────
  # Use this pattern when your repo must consume something through a specific
  # abstraction layer (SDK, interface, adapter) and never bypass it.

  - id: no_direct_rpc_calls
    claim: "This repo accesses on-chain state through the SDK — not via direct RPC"
    source: codebase
    severity: FROZEN
    verify:
      search: "ethers JsonRpcProvider WebSocketProvider direct rpc"
    why: >
      Direct RPC calls bypass SDK-level retry logic, fallback handling, and
      type safety. Any direct call here is a divergence from the reference
      architecture and a source of silent failures under load.
    # ↑ REPLACE with your actual dependency boundary.
    # Common variants: "accesses DB through the ORM", "calls external APIs
    # through the gateway", "reads config through the config module".


  # ── EXAMPLE 2: Interface contract ───────────────────────────────────────
  # Use this pattern when this repo exposes or consumes an interface that
  # another repo depends on. This is your cross-repo coordination surface.

  - id: event_schema_stable
    claim: "Emitted events match the schema declared in /contracts/events.ts"
    source: codebase
    severity: FROZEN
    verify:
      search: "emit EventName schema contracts/events"
    why: >
      Downstream repos (indexer, SDK) parse these events. An undeclared schema
      change here breaks them silently — no compiler error, no test failure,
      just wrong data in production. Schema changes must be declared here
      before the PR lands.
    # ↑ REPLACE with your actual interface contract.
    # Ask: "What does another repo assume about what this repo produces?"
    # That assumption belongs here as an assert.


  # ── EXAMPLE 3: Scope boundary ───────────────────────────────────────────
  # Use this pattern to record what this repo explicitly does NOT do.
  # Especially useful on grant projects where scope creep kills deadlines.

  - id: no_auth_logic_in_this_repo
    claim: "Authentication and authorization logic lives in the auth service — not here"
    source: codebase
    severity: VERSIONED
    verify:
      search: "jwt verify authenticate authorize token middleware"
    why: >
      Auth was explicitly moved out of scope in milestone 1. If auth logic
      reappears here, it means a scope assumption has silently changed.
      That needs a conversation, not a commit.
    # ↑ REPLACE with your actual out-of-scope boundary.
    # Useful on EF grant projects: "This repo does not implement X —
    # that is milestone 3 scope." Write it down before someone builds it.


  # ── EXAMPLE 4: Operational invariant ────────────────────────────────────
  # Use this pattern for things that must remain true about how the repo
  # behaves at runtime — not just at build time.

  - id: no_unbounded_queries
    claim: "All database queries include a limit — no unbounded result sets"
    source: codebase
    severity: VERSIONED
    verify:
      search: "findMany find query limit pagination"
    why: >
      Unbounded queries are a latent performance failure. They pass in dev,
      fail in staging, and take down prod. This repo's query patterns are
      the responsibility of this maintainer.
    # ↑ REPLACE with your actual operational constraint.
    # Other common variants: "all external calls have a timeout",
    # "no synchronous file I/O in request handlers".


  # ── EXAMPLE 5: Advisory / drift detection ───────────────────────────────
  # Use this pattern for things you want to notice but not block on.
  # Good for surfacing slow drift before it becomes a crisis.

  - id: test_coverage_not_regressing
    claim: "Test coverage for the core module stays above 70%"
    source: codebase
    severity: ADDITIVE
    verify:
      search: "coverage core module test"
    why: >
      We are not enforcing 100% coverage. We are noticing if it falls
      below the floor that makes refactoring safe. Below 70% on core logic,
      we are flying blind.
    # ↑ REPLACE with your actual quality floor.
    # ADVISORY asserts don't block — they show up in the watcher report
    # and surface in triage. That's the point.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BEFORE YOU SUBMIT THIS FILE FOR REVIEW
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Check each assert against this list:
#
#   [ ] The claim is falsifiable — it can fail
#   [ ] The severity is honest — FROZEN means any change forks the protocol,
#       VERSIONED means a migration plan is required, ADDITIVE means nothing breaks
#   [ ] The `why` explains the consequence, not just the rule
#   [ ] The `verify.search` terms would actually find a violation
#   [ ] You would notice within 24h if this assert started failing
#
# If you're unsure whether something belongs here — add it as ADVISORY.
# It costs nothing to watch. It costs a lot to miss.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
